# Deterministic test backends

`mockai` and `mockcurl` are the only AI backends used by the test suite. They
never contact a model or a non-loopback host.

Build them as standalone strict C11/POSIX programs:

    cc -std=c11 -pedantic -Wall -Wextra -Werror -O2 \
       -o build/tests/helpers/mockai tests/helpers/mockai.c
    cc -std=c11 -pedantic -Wall -Wextra -Werror -O2 \
       -o build/tests/helpers/mockcurl tests/helpers/mockcurl.c

Run the HTTP backend with a loopback ephemeral port:

    build/tests/helpers/mockai --port 0 --script tests/fixtures/ai/openai.script

It prints `port N`, flushes stdout, and replays the script for every accepted
connection. `--seed N` is accepted for deterministic seeded scenarios; the
base grammar itself contains no randomness.

`mockcurl` has curl's yew-facing command line (`-sS --no-buffer --config -`).
Set `YEW_AI_MOCK_SCRIPT` to the script path. It reads and validates the curl
config on stdin, requires the URL, POST method, JSON body, JSON content type,
write-out marker, and an Authorization or x-api-key header, then replays the
body and prints the scripted status marker on stderr. `--version` reports a
supported deterministic curl version.

## Script grammar

Scripts are UTF-8 line-oriented text. Blank lines and lines beginning with
`#` are ignored. Directives execute in file order:

    status 200
    header content-type: text/event-stream
    framing sse
    delay 100
    split 1
    emit data: {"choices":[]}\n\n
    split 0
    chunk literal bytes\n
    close
    exit 7

- `status N` sets the HTTP status (default 200).
- `header NAME: VALUE` appends a response header.
- `framing sse|ndjson|length|close|chunked` selects response framing.
  `sse` and `ndjson` add their content type when absent and are
  close-delimited; `length` computes Content-Length; `chunked` adds
  Transfer-Encoding and a final zero chunk unless `close` runs.
- `delay N` waits N milliseconds before the next operation.
- `emit BYTES` writes exactly BYTES.
- `chunk BYTES` writes an HTTP chunk around BYTES for `mockai`; `mockcurl`
  emits the decoded BYTES, matching real curl.
- `split 1` makes subsequent writes one byte at a time; `split 0` restores
  whole-buffer writes.
- `close` ends the response immediately without an implicit terminator.
- `exit N` selects `mockcurl`'s exit status and has no effect on `mockai`.

The byte arguments decode only `\\n`, `\\r`, `\\t`, `\\\\`, and `\\xNN`.
Every other byte is copied verbatim. This deliberately small escape language
makes response bytes identical across compilers and runs.
